# Copy to .env and fill in what you need. Nothing here is required: `make`
# runs offline, with no key and no database, and every optional piece that is
# missing turns into a named skip rather than a failure.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored and must stay that way. Nothing in this file is a real
# value.
#
# The two API keys are placeholders to replace. Every other line is already
# set to what the system does when the variable is absent: the code's own
# default where it has one, and empty where the code reads empty and absent
# the same way. So copying this file changes nothing until you edit a line,
# and each default is written as a value rather than described in prose. Two
# lines are commented, because for them no value means the same as leaving
# them out; each says why. tests/test_env_example.py holds every other line to
# this, and CI fails if the code reads a variable that has no line here.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- the model
# Only read by `make live` and `python -m tools.live`. Without one of these the
# system drafts with its rule-following reference reasoner, which needs no key
# and no network, and the rest of the pipeline is identical.

# Default provider. Has free models; `python -m tools.live --list-free` lists
# the ones answering right now.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-your-key-here

# Only for --provider anthropic.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-key-here

# The alternative to the key when a gateway issues short-lived tokens; set one
# or the other. Commented rather than empty because the Anthropic SDK reads it
# directly, outside this codebase, so whether an empty token counts as no
# token is the SDK's decision and not one this file can promise.
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=your-gateway-token

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- the store
# With no database reachable the store falls back to append-only JSONL files
# and says which backend answered, on the page and at /health.

# Postgres. `docker compose up` starts one at this address, and it is also the
# address tried when this is unset.
CLINICIAN_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://clinician:clinician@localhost:5544/clinician

# Where the file backend writes.
CLINICIAN_STORE=.store

# `files` stays on disk even with a database running. Useful for a laptop, and
# set by the test suite so that no test can write into a real deployment's
# tables. Empty uses Postgres whenever it answers.
CLINICIAN_STORE_BACKEND=

# `1` re-runs work even when a stored result would match. The second `make` is
# fast because it replays; this is how you make it do the work again.
CLINICIAN_FRESH=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ the deployment

# Interface to bind. A public deployment sets 0.0.0.0, and the container does.
HOST=127.0.0.1

# Port to listen on. A platform usually sets this itself, and then it wins over
# the default of 8000. --port wins over both.
PORT=

# `1` marks a public deployment. The page says so, and the reset button is
# refused unless CLINICIAN_ALLOW_RESET says otherwise.
CLINICIAN_HOSTED=

# Whether the button that destroys the store is allowed. Left out, it is on
# locally and off on a hosted deployment; `1` allows it everywhere and `0`
# refuses it everywhere. Commented rather than empty because any value at all,
# an empty one included, overrides that default instead of restating it.
# CLINICIAN_ALLOW_RESET=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the logs
# Structured JSON on stderr. There is no hosted log drain and no SaaS error
# tracker by design: a stack trace here can carry the record that produced it,
# and health data must be processed in-country.

# debug | info | warning | error. Empty leaves it to --log-level, then info.
# A value here beats --log-level, deliberately: it lets an operator raise the
# level on a running deployment without changing the command.
CLINICIAN_LOG_LEVEL=

# `plain` makes them readable for a person. Empty is JSON, for a machine.
CLINICIAN_LOG_FORMAT=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the validator
# The official HL7 FHIR validator jar, 190 MB, fetched once into .tools/ by
# `python -m tools.validate_fhir --download`. Set this only to use a jar kept
# somewhere else. Empty tries --jar, then the downloaded one, and with neither
# the run says so and skips rather than failing.
FHIR_VALIDATOR_JAR=
